# GitHub Workflow (compulsory)

All work follows this sequence. Scope: all work.

## 1. Issue first

Create a GitHub issue using the **Bounded Context Work Item** template (`.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/bounded-context.yml`) before any code changes. If the user requests work and no issue exists, create the issue first—never start coding without an issue.

**Body structure (API-created issues):**
```
## Background

[why this is needed]

## Expected outcome

[clear, testable outcome]

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Criterion 1
- [ ] Criterion 2

## Possible solution(s)

1. Option A - ...
2. Option B - ...

## References

- Link to doc
- Related issue #N
```
Title format `type(scope): description` provides type and scope for labels.

- Title format: `type(scope): description` (e.g. `feat(web): add validation`, `chore(tooling): add commitlint`)
- Labels `type` and `scope` are auto-applied from the title
- Assignee: creator is auto-assigned (commissioning developer)

## 2. Branch

Checkout/create branch from `main` using `fix/123-slug` or `feat/123-slug` (issue number + short slug).

## 3. Plan

When an agent creates an execution plan (e.g. todo list), post it as a comment on the issue before starting work.

## 4. Work

Make changes within the bounded context of the issue. When contracts change: run codegen in same PR and tick "Regeneration Required: yes" in PR template.

## 5. Commits

Produce a series of commits—one per small block of work. Each commit must use conventional format (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `docs:` etc.). Atomic and reviewable.

## 6. PR

Rebase on `main`, then open PR targeting `main`. Use same title format as issue: `type(scope): description`. Assign the PR to the **git user** (the GitHub user who owns the branch; e.g. `gh pr create --assignee @me`). Fill PR template (Summary, Contracts Changed, Regeneration Required, Validation). Include `Resolves #123` in description.

## 7. Checks

Ensure all CI checks pass before marking work complete. Use `mcp_GitHub_pull_request_read` with `method: get_status` to verify. Re-run or fix failures.

## 8. Post check passing

Resolve all review comments with follow-up—fix the issue or explain why it doesn't need to be addressed. Add a PR comment summarizing how each comment was handled (fixed or why not addressed).
